#===============================================================================
# ai_projectile_evade.zss
#
# Universal CPU anti-fireball jump. One shared rule for the whole roster, no
# per-character edits, no engine rebuild. Registered in save/config.ini under
# [Common] States (LAST in the list - see "LOAD ORDER" below).
#
# WHAT IT DOES
#
#   A CPU-controlled character that sees an ENEMY projectile heading for it
#   jumps over it - straight up, or up+forward when the opponent is far enough
#   away that a forward jump is the punish. It does not care what the
#   projectile's ID is, which character threw it, or which state threw it: the
#   enemy's whole live projectile list is scanned every frame.
#
#   Characters that answer a fireball with a fireball of their own are EXEMPT
#   (see EXEMPT ROSTER). They keep their own AI - except against a HYPER
#   projectile, which a special-level projectile cannot trade with, so they jump
#   over those like everybody else.
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY IT IS SHAPED LIKE THIS - the two problems, and what solves each
#
# 1. "OVERRIDE THE CHARACTER'S OWN AI"
#
#    Every .cmd in this roster carries its own AI block inside [Statedef -1],
#    and a ChangeState there STOPS the rest of state -1 for that frame
#    (bytecode.go, changeState.Run returns "stop" for a self-change; StateBlock
#    .Run propagates it out of the whole state). Common state files are compiled
#    AFTER the character's own files and their negative states are APPENDED
#    (compiler.go), so a [StateDef -1] here would sit behind the character's AI
#    and simply never run on any frame where that AI acted.
#
#    That is not a rare frame. Adon's AI, for one, is:
#
#        [State -3]; Guard      (label is cosmetic - it is under Statedef -1)
#        type = ChangeState
#        value = 130
#        triggerall = var(59)
#        triggerall = statetype != A && ctrl
#        trigger2 = EnemyNear, NumProj || ProjContact = 1
#
#    - "the enemy has ANY projectile out" with no distance test at all, so it
#    fires on EVERY frame the character has ctrl while a fireball is on screen.
#    Exactly the window this file needs. Most of the roster is built the same
#    way.
#
#    The fix is the hook: [StateDef +1] (state -10). src/char.go actionRun runs
#    it AFTER -4/-3/-2/-1, after the buffered state change, and after the
#    current state - and it is not reachable by any ChangeState the character
#    made, because it is a separate state block. If it changes state, actionRun
#    applies that immediately and runs the new state in the same frame. It is
#    the one hook a character's own AI cannot take away.
#
# 2. "BUT AT +1 THE CHARACTER NO LONGER HAS CTRL"
#
#    By the time +1 runs, the AI has already moved the character into state 130
#    (guard) or into an attack, so testing "ctrl" there always fails. So the
#    eligibility test is taken in [StateDef -4], which runs BEFORE -1, and
#    parked in map(_sfaz_pje_ready). +1 acts on what was true before the
#    character's AI got its turn.
#
#    Note that this also means a move the AI started THIS frame is cancelled on
#    its very first tick, before anything is drawn - which is the intended
#    "override". A move that was already running (no ctrl at -4 time) is never
#    interrupted.
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# HOW A PROJECTILE IS FOUND - and why nothing here mentions an ID
#
#   enemy(n), NumProj             -> how many live projectiles that enemy owns
#   enemy(n), ProjVar(-1, i, ...) -> read projectile #i, ANY id (-1 = wildcard,
#                                    char.go getMultipleProjs: "id < 0" matches)
#
# ProjVar is the Ikemen trigger that makes this possible at all; plain Mugen can
# only count an enemy's projectiles, never locate them. Everything is read in
# OUR local coordinate space (the engine divides by the reading character's
# localscl), so no scaling maths is needed even between chars with different
# localcoord.
#
# Two conventions that are easy to get wrong, both verified in src:
#   * ProjVar pos x is camera-relative, and so is the "pos x" trigger, so
#     subtracting them gives a true world offset.
#   * ProjVar vel x is FACING-RELATIVE (char.go: pos += velocity[0] * facing).
#     World velocity is therefore vel x * ProjVar facing. Same convention as the
#     character's own "vel x", which is why "closing speed contributed by my own
#     movement" is simply "vel x" with no facing term at all.
#
# HELPER-THROWN projectiles are covered without doing anything special: a helper
# only owns its projectiles if it was created with ownprojectile (char.go
# canOwnProjectiles); nothing in this roster sets that, so every projectile -
# including Bison's and N-Bison's helper-spawned 3001 hyper, Cody's 1905 and
# Rose's 19001 - is owned by the root and shows up in enemy(n), NumProj.
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE DECISION - simulate the jump, do not guess a time window
#
# THE BUG THIS REPLACES, because it will be tempting to "simplify" back to it:
# the first version picked a fixed window of ticks-to-impact (jump if impact is
# 16-36 ticks away), where ticks-to-impact was computed from the character's
# CURRENT velocity. That is wrong twice over, and the symptom was exactly what
# it sounds like - characters taking a short hop forward into the fireball and
# air-blocking it:
#
#   * A jump REPLACES the character's horizontal velocity. A forward jump adds
#     velocity.jump.fwd.x (2.9 for Zangief, 4.3 for Vega) to the closing speed,
#     so against a 3-unit-a-frame hadouken the projectile arrives almost TWICE
#     as fast as the standing estimate said. A character that triggered at the
#     late edge of the window was hit at jump tick 6, barely off the ground.
#   * A fixed tick window cannot fit the whole roster anyway. Zangief rises
#     7.8/frame decaying at .43 and peaks at 74 units; Vega rises 11.5 at .60
#     and peaks at 116. The same 16 ticks is a very different height.
#
# So this version simulates instead. Everything below comes from the character's
# OWN constants, so it is correct per character with nothing to tune per char:
#
#     jy    = -const(velocity.jump.y)      rise speed, as a positive number
#     ya    =  const(movement.yaccel)      gravity
#     apex  =  jy / ya                     ticks to the top of the jump
#     h(t)  =  jy*t - ya*t*(t-1)/2         height above the ground at tick t
#                                          (the discrete sum the engine actually
#                                          performs, not the calculus version)
#
# For a candidate jump with horizontal speed jx toward the enemy:
#
#     rem = ahead - (projectile speed + my current forward speed) * JUMP_STARTUP
#     t   = rem / (projectile speed + jx)      ticks airborne when it arrives
#
# and the jump is taken only when BOTH
#
#     t <= apex          impact happens while still rising, never on the way down
#     h(t) >= CLEAR      and high enough to actually be over it
#
# The "t <= apex" half is what sets the timing, and it is self-tuning: as the
# projectile approaches, t falls. The FIRST frame the test passes is the frame
# where t equals apex - i.e. the character jumps at the exact moment that puts
# the projectile underneath it at the TOP of its arc. There is no window to
# tune, and no way to trigger at an edge where the arc is too low.
#
# Worked example, Zangief vs a light hadouken (3 units a frame): apex is 18.1
# ticks, so a forward jump is taken at ahead = 18.1 * (3 + 2.9) + 3*4 = 119
# units, and a straight-up one at ahead = 18.1 * 3 + 12 = 66. The old code
# triggered anywhere from 48 units out, which is where the hop-into-it came from.
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TUNABLES - everything worth changing is here. Search the CAPITALISED name.
#
#   JUMP_STARTUP   4   ticks between the decision and leaving the ground, for a
#                      jump this file started: 3 for the jump-start state plus
#                      the 1 tick State +1 costs to react. Exact, because the
#                      3 is pinned - see PIN THE JUMP START in State +1.
#   JUMP_STARTUP_MAX 9 the same thing for a jump the character's own AI started
#                      on the frame we wanted one, where the length is whatever
#                      that character's animations make it. The apex test uses
#                      the SHORT bound and the clearance test the LONG one, so
#                      the jump is safe anywhere in between. Underestimating is
#                      the dangerous direction: a longer start means less time
#                      airborne when the projectile arrives, i.e. lower on the
#                      arc, which is exactly how Ken got clipped at 34 units.
#   CLEAR              how high the character must be when the projectile
#                      arrives: (-projectile y at impact) + const240p(5), i.e.
#                      just over its centre line. Derived from the boxes: a
#                      hadouken's Clsn1 is -20,-4,9,13 around its own position,
#                      and an airborne character's Clsn2 bottom sits anywhere
#                      from 11 BELOW its position (Ryu) to 46 above it (Cody),
#                      so its own centre line is the honest middle. This is a
#                      FEASIBILITY test more than a timing one - because the
#                      trigger targets the apex, the real clearance is the peak
#                      of the arc, and CLEAR is what stops a character whose
#                      jump simply cannot get over a given projectile.
#   HIT_Y_LOW     10   impact heights BELOW this are ignored (positive y is
#                      below ground level).
#   HIT_Y_HIGH         const(size.head.pos.y) - const240p(20): a projectile that
#                      would pass over a STANDING character's head is not a
#                      threat and is ignored.
#   CLEAR              how high the character must be when the projectile
#                      arrives: (-projectile y at impact) - const240p(8).
#                      Derived from the boxes, NOT guessed. A hadouken's Clsn1 is
#                      -20,-4,9,13 about its own position, so its top edge is 4
#                      above that position; and an airborne character's Clsn2
#                      BOTTOM sits above its own position for all but four of the
#                      roster (median -25, and -80 for Dee Jay; only Ryu, E-Ryu,
#                      Ken and V-Ken hang below, at about +10). So clearing it
#                      needs -projY + 4 + boxBottom, and const240p(8) ~ -10 is a
#                      cautious stand-in for that box bottom.
#
#                      This is the number that decides WHO can use the system at
#                      all, so it is worth being accurate rather than safe: it
#                      gates on peak jump height, and the roster's peaks run from
#                      Birdie's 64 and Zangief's 75 to Vega's 116. An earlier
#                      value of +const240p(5) (i.e. assuming the hurtbox bottom
#                      sat AT the character's position) put the bar at 58 and
#                      quietly locked out the bottom of that range - playtesting
#                      reported Birdie jumping about 5% of fireballs, Zangief
#                      "mostly", Blanka and Vega perfect, which is exactly that
#                      list in order of jump height.
#   FWD_DIST      90   (240p units) opponent farther than this -> up+forward
#                      jump; closer -> straight up, so the character does not
#                      jump into them.
#
# Distances that are not read from the character's own constants use
# const240p(), so they mean the same thing on a 320-wide char as on the 384-wide
# ones in this roster.
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY "STRAIGHT UP" NEEDS THE VELOCITY PINNED
#
# Mode 1 uses common1.cns state 40, and state 40 decides its own horizontal
# velocity from the INPUT:
#
#     [State 40, 2] VarSet trigger1 = command = "holdfwd"   sysvar(1) = 1
#     [State 40, 4] VelSet x = ifelse(sysvar(1)=0, neu.x, ifelse(...fwd.x...))
#
# An AI's inputs are randomised, so if it happens to be holding forward that
# frame the "straight up" jump silently becomes a forward one - and the model
# just finished computing the arc for a velocity the jump is not going to use.
# PIN THE JUMP START in State +1 sets the velocity itself from the same constant
# the model read, so mode 1 is genuinely straight up on every character.
#
# Writing a private neutral-jump statedef instead was rejected: Vega picks its
# jump animations as 40 + var(29)*10000 (claw / clawless), so a generic state
# cannot pick the right sprites for the whole roster. Entering the character's
# own 40/42 and only overriding the velocity keeps every per-character animation
# rule intact.
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# EXEMPT ROSTER - characters that can trade fireballs
#
# Matched on the "name" trigger (the [Info] name, NOT displayname - several
# characters share a displayname). Comparison is case-insensitive; the engine
# lowercases both sides.
#
#   Ryu           "Ryu"              SFZ_Ryu
#                 "Evil Ryu"         SFZ_E-Ryu
#   Ken           "Ken"              SFZ_Ken
#                 "Violent Ken"      SFZ_V-Ken
#   Chun-Li       "X-ISM ChunLi"     SFZ_ChunLi-X
#                 "Z-ISM ChunLi"     SFZ_ChunLi-Z
#   Sakura        "Sakura Kasugano"  SFZ_Sakura
#                 "Sakura Dark"      SFZ_D-Sakura
#   Charlie       "Charlie Nash"     SFZ_Charlie AND SFZ_R-Charlie (same name)
#   Akuma         "Gouki"            SFZ_Akuma
#                 "Shin_Akuma"       SFZ_AkumaS
#   M.Bison       "Bison"            SFZ_Bison   - and ONLY this one. N-Bison's
#                                    [Info] name is "Dictador", so it is not
#                                    matched and it does jump, as asked.
#   Rose          "Rose"             SFZ_Rose
#   Guile         "William Guile"    SFZ_Guile
#   Dhalsim       "Dhalsim"          SFZ_Dhalsim
#
# The exemption is lifted for a HYPER projectile, detected by hit attribute
# (ProjVar attr = SCA, HP). Checked against the data: every hyper projectile in
# this roster is declared "attr = <S|A>, HP" and every special-level one "SP",
# so the attribute alone separates them - no state-number lists needed.
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE JUMP ITSELF
#
#   mode 1 -> state 40, the normal jump start (see the note above about state 40
#             reading the input for its direction).
#   mode 2 -> state 42, "Jump (CPU)" - already in common1.cns and already used
#             by this roster's own AI (Adon's [State -1, Nomal Jump] and 20-odd
#             others). It applies velocity.jump.fwd.x unconditionally, so the
#             direction cannot be lost to whatever the AI happens to be holding.
#
# Both exist for every character: all 48 use stcommon = common1.cns, and the
# five that define their own 40 (Cody x2, Dhalsim, Juni, Vega) all define a
# normal jump start. Only Vega defines its own 42, also a CPU forward jump.
#
# moveType is forced to I first. Statedefs 40 and 42 do not declare a movetype,
# so cancelling an attack into them would otherwise carry moveType = A into the
# jump, leaving the character "attacking" with no HitDef.
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# LOAD ORDER
#
# This file must be LAST in [Common] States. data/system.zss also defines
# [StateDef +1] and negative / "+1" statedefs from several common files are
# appended in load order, so being last means this block runs after it. Nothing
# in system.zss's +1 changes state, so the two do not fight - but keep the order
# anyway if anything is ever added there.
#===============================================================================


[StateDef -4]

#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Runs before the character's own -3/-2/-1, and for helpers too, hence the gate.
# teamSide = 0 is a stage character.
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
if !isHelper && teamSide != 0 {

	map(_sfaz_pje_mode)  := 0;
	map(_sfaz_pje_ready) := 0;

	# PROTECT THE ARC. Without this the jump is not the jump that was modelled.
	# One tick after leaving the ground the character turns its own jump into an
	# air guard, and the arc collapses. Measured on Zangief: peak 39 instead of
	# 74, hit on the way up. That is the "short jump forward that doesn't clear
	# it".
	#
	# There are two mechanisms and they need different answers:
	#
	#   * The ENGINE's automatic guard (char.go actionPrepare) sees back held
	#     plus inguarddist and sends the character to state 120. The guard
	#     AssertSpecial flags stop that.
	#   * The character's OWN AI, which the flags cannot touch because it is a
	#     plain ChangeState. Zangief's .cmd:
	#
	#         [State -3]; type = ChangeState; value = 132   ; air guard
	#         triggerall = var(59)
	#         triggerall = inguarddist
	#         trigger1 = statetype = A && ctrl
	#
	#     State 132 is physics = N and applies gravity ITSELF with a VelAdd; it
	#     then falls straight through to 140 ("stop guarding", because the AI is
	#     not holding back), whose [State 140,4] puts physics back to A. So the
	#     character bounces 140 -> 132 -> 140 every single frame and gravity is
	#     applied TWICE per frame - .86 instead of .43. That is the doubled decay,
	#     and it is why the arc peaks at barely half its height.
	#
	# The common factor those AI triggers all need is CTRL, so taking ctrl away
	# for the rising half is what actually beats them - and it is taken away in
	# -4, before the character's own -1 gets to look at it. Control is handed
	# back at the apex, so the descent behaves completely normally, air guard
	# included. Only a jump this file asked for is ever touched (see the latch in
	# State +1); every other jump the character takes is left alone.
	if gameTime < map(_sfaz_pje_air) && stateType = A && moveType != H && !inCustomState {
		if vel y < 0 {
			assertSpecial{flag: noAirGuard; flag2: noStandGuard; flag3: noCrouchGuard}
			ctrlSet{value: 0}
		} else {
			# Apex. Stop protecting and give control back.
			ctrlSet{value: 1}
			map(_sfaz_pje_air) := 0;
		}
	}

	# ELIGIBILITY. Read here and only here: at +1 the character's AI may already
	# have spent its ctrl on a guard or an attack.
	if roundState = 2 && aiLevel > 0 && alive && !inCustomState && ctrl
			&& stateType != A && moveType != H
			&& gameVar(pausetime) = 0 && gameVar(superpausetime) = 0 {
		map(_sfaz_pje_ready) := 1;
	}

	if map(_sfaz_pje_ready) != 0 {

		# EXEMPT ROSTER (see header). Split off into a var rather than folded into
		# the scan, so the string compares happen once per frame and not once per
		# projectile.
		let pjeExempt = 0;
		if name = "Ryu" || name = "Evil Ryu"
				|| name = "Ken" || name = "Violent Ken"
				|| name = "X-ISM ChunLi" || name = "Z-ISM ChunLi"
				|| name = "Sakura Kasugano" || name = "Sakura Dark"
				|| name = "Charlie Nash"
				|| name = "Gouki" || name = "Shin_Akuma"
				|| name = "Bison"
				|| name = "Rose"
				|| name = "William Guile"
				|| name = "Dhalsim" {
			let pjeExempt = 1;
		}

		# This character's own jump arc, from its own constants.
		let pjeJY   = -const(velocity.jump.y);          # rise speed, positive
		let pjeYA   = max(0.05, const(movement.yaccel));
		let pjeApex = $pjeJY / $pjeYA;                  # ticks to the top
		let pjeJFX  = const(velocity.jump.fwd.x);       # forward jump speed
		let pjeJNX  = const(velocity.jump.neu.x);       # normally 0

		let pjeMyX   = pos x;
		let pjeVF    = max(0, vel x);                   # my own closing speed
		let pjeYHigh = const(size.head.pos.y) - const240p(20);   # HIT_Y_HIGH

		# Forward jump only when the opponent is not already on top of us.
		let pjeFar = 0;
		if p2BodyDist x >= const240p(90) {              # FWD_DIST
			let pjeFar = 1;
		}

		let pjeMode = 0;
		let pjeNE   = numEnemy;

		# Every enemy, every one of its live projectiles, any ID.
		#
		# Each redirected read is assigned on its own line: an operator may not
		# appear at the top level of a redirection ("'-' operator cannot be used
		# within a trigger redirection"), so all the arithmetic is done on the
		# locals afterwards.
		for pjeE = 0; $pjeNE - 1; 1 {
			let pjeNP = enemy($pjeE), numProj;
			for pjeI = 0; $pjeNP - 1; 1 {

				let pjePX    = enemy($pjeE), projVar(-1, $pjeI, pos x);
				let pjeAhead = ($pjePX - $pjeMyX) * facing;   # > 0 = in front of us

				if $pjeAhead > 0 {
					let pjePVX = enemy($pjeE), projVar(-1, $pjeI, vel x);
					let pjePF  = enemy($pjeE), projVar(-1, $pjeI, facing);
					# vel x is facing-relative on both sides; the trailing * facing
					# turns it into "speed toward me".
					let pjeSpd = -($pjePVX * $pjePF) * facing;

					if $pjeSpd > 0.05 {
						let pjePY  = enemy($pjeE), projVar(-1, $pjeI, pos y);
						let pjePVY = enemy($pjeE), projVar(-1, $pjeI, vel y);

						# Is it a threat at all? Answered for a character that
						# does NOT move: where would it be when it got here?
						# Discards the one that sails overhead and the air
						# fireball that arcs down and lands short.
						let pjeT0 = $pjeAhead / max(0.05, $pjeSpd + $pjeVF);
						let pjeY0 = $pjePY + $pjePVY * $pjeT0;

						if $pjeY0 <= const240p(10) && $pjeY0 >= $pjeYHigh {

							# Distance still to cover once we are off the ground.
							# TWO bounds, because the jump start is not a fixed
							# length - see JUMP_STARTUP in the header.
							let pjeRemA = $pjeAhead - ($pjeSpd + $pjeVF) * 4;
							let pjeRemC = $pjeAhead - ($pjeSpd + $pjeVF) * 9;

							if $pjeRemC > 0 {
								# Ticks airborne at impact, and the height of the
								# arc then, for each candidate jump. The jump
								# REPLACES our horizontal velocity, which is the
								# whole point of computing this per mode.
								# ...A = shortest start (most airborne time, used
								# for the apex test); ...C = longest start (least
								# airborne time, used for the clearance test).
								let pjeTAF = $pjeRemA / max(0.05, $pjeSpd + $pjeJFX);
								let pjeTCF = $pjeRemC / max(0.05, $pjeSpd + $pjeJFX);
								let pjeTAN = $pjeRemA / max(0.05, $pjeSpd + $pjeJNX);
								let pjeTCN = $pjeRemC / max(0.05, $pjeSpd + $pjeJNX);

								let pjeHF = $pjeJY * $pjeTCF - $pjeYA * $pjeTCF * ($pjeTCF - 1) / 2;
								let pjeHN = $pjeJY * $pjeTCN - $pjeYA * $pjeTCN * ($pjeTCN - 1) / 2;

								# CLEAR, evaluated where the projectile will be.
								let pjeCF = -($pjePY + $pjePVY * ($pjeTCF + 9)) - const240p(8);
								let pjeCN = -($pjePY + $pjePVY * ($pjeTCN + 9)) - const240p(8);

								# Impact must land on the way UP even with the
								# shortest start, and be high enough even with the
								# longest. See "THE DECISION" in the header.
								let pjeOkF = 0;
								if $pjeTAF <= $pjeApex && $pjeHF >= $pjeCF {
									let pjeOkF = 1;
								}
								let pjeOkN = 0;
								if $pjeTAN <= $pjeApex && $pjeHN >= $pjeCN {
									let pjeOkN = 1;
								}

								let pjeThreat = 0;
								if $pjeExempt = 0 {
									let pjeThreat = 1;
								} else if enemy($pjeE), projVar(-1, $pjeI, attr) = SCA, HP {
									# Exempt characters still jump hypers: a
									# special-level projectile cannot trade with one.
									let pjeThreat = 1;
								}

								if $pjeThreat != 0 {
									if $pjeFar != 0 && $pjeOkF != 0 {
										let pjeMode = 2;            # up + forward
									} else if $pjeOkN != 0 && $pjeMode = 0 {
										let pjeMode = 1;            # straight up
									}
								}
							}
						}
					}
				}
			}
		}

		map(_sfaz_pje_mode) := $pjeMode;
	}
}


[StateDef +1]

#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The actuator. Runs after -1 and after the current state, so whatever the
# character's own AI did this frame, this gets the last word. State -4 already
# decided; this only checks that the frame did not turn into something where a
# jump would be wrong (got hit, thrown, KO'd, or the AI happened to jump by
# itself).
#
# ctrl is deliberately NOT re-tested here - see WHY IT IS SHAPED LIKE THIS (2).
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
if !isHelper && teamSide != 0 && map(_sfaz_pje_mode) != 0 && map(_sfaz_pje_ready) != 0 {

	map(_sfaz_pje_ready) := 0;

	# NO JUMPING OUT OF A CUSTOM COMBO. This used to read "!isAsserted(noJump)",
	# on the reasoning that data/custom_combo.zss asserted noJump for the whole
	# duration - but it has since stopped doing that for a human and asserts it
	# only for the AI, so the proxy changed meaning twice without this file being
	# touched. The mode is tested directly now, by its own map key, which is what
	# was always meant: spending the mode hopping over a fireball wastes it.
	if alive && !inCustomState && moveType != H && map(_sfaz_cc_active) = 0 {

		# Hold the arc open for the rising half - see PROTECT THE ARC in -4.
		# JUMP_PROTECT 45 ticks is a comfortable upper bound on any rise in this
		# roster (the longest apex is Ryu's at 21), and it is only an upper
		# bound: "vel y <= 0" ends it at the apex on its own.
		map(_sfaz_pje_air) := gameTime + 45;
		map(_sfaz_pje_atk) := gameTime + 70;   # AIR_PUNISH window (whole jump)

		# stateNo 40/42/43/44/45/50 = the jump states. Reaching +1 already in one
		# of them means the character's own AI jumped on the very frame we wanted
		# a jump - which happens often, because both are reacting to the same
		# fireball. Do NOT start a second jump; the latch above is set either way,
		# so the arc is protected whoever started it. Leaving this case
		# unprotected was why half the jumps still came up short.
		# AND NOT OUT OF A HYPER. +1 runs AFTER the current state (src/char.go
		# actionRun: -4, -3, -2, -1, stateChange2, the state itself, then +1 as
		# states[-10]), so on the first frame of a hyper its own SuperPause has
		# already run and already taken the level. Jumping out from here spends the
		# bar and gives nothing back. The "nothing is drawn yet" argument in (2)
		# above is true for a normal and false for a hyper. Same defect as the
		# REACH_HYPER clause removed from data/ai_offense.zss - see the note there.
		# A fireball eaten at full meter is cheaper than a level thrown away.
		if stateType != A && stateNo != [3000, 4999]
				&& stateNo != 40 && stateNo != 42 && stateNo != 43
				&& stateNo != 44 && stateNo != 45 && stateNo != 50 {

			# Statedefs 40 and 42 declare no movetype, so an attack cancelled
			# into them would stay moveType = A.
			stateTypeSet{movetype: I}

			# Diagnostic latch: the gameTime of the last jump this file forced.
			# Nothing reads it in-game; it is here so a Lua probe or the debug
			# console can tell one of these jumps apart from the character's own.
			map(_sfaz_pje_fired) := gameTime;

			# Both latches MUST be written before the ChangeState. A
			# ChangeState on self returns "stop", which ends the whole state
			# block - anything after it in this file simply never runs.
			map(_sfaz_pje_pin) := gameTime + 12;

			if map(_sfaz_pje_mode) = 2 {
				map(_sfaz_pje_vx) := const(velocity.jump.fwd.x);
				changeState{value: 42}                        # CPU forward jump
			} else {
				map(_sfaz_pje_vx) := const(velocity.jump.neu.x);
				changeState{value: 40}                        # neutral jump
			}
		}
	}
}

#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PIN THE JUMP START, for a jump this file started (map(_sfaz_pje_pin)).
#
# States 40 and 42 leave on "AnimTime = 0", which is NOT a fixed number of ticks.
# Anim 40 is a single 3-tick frame for most of the roster, but Dhalsim's is 2,
# Honda's and Dee Jay's are 6, and state 42 does not even ask for anim 40 - it
# asks for "ifelse(!var(20) || var(20) = 1, 40, 50)". Ken has no anim 50 at all,
# so that ChangeAnim does nothing and the state leaves whenever whatever anim the
# character happened to be playing runs out. Measured: Ken sat in state 42 for
# EIGHT ticks, the fireball arrived five ticks earlier in the arc than the model
# assumed, and he was clipped at 34 units up. Bison's anim 50 is 33 ticks.
#
# So for our own jumps the start is pinned to 3 ticks and the horizontal velocity
# is set from the same constant the model used - which also makes the "straight
# up" mode genuinely straight up, instead of state 40 reading whatever direction
# the AI's randomised input happened to be holding.
#
# Jumps the character's own AI started are NOT pinned (we did not choose their
# velocity either); they are only protected, and JUMP_STARTUP_MAX in the model is
# what covers their unknown length.
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
if !isHelper && teamSide != 0 && gameTime < map(_sfaz_pje_pin)
		&& stateType = S && moveType != H && !inCustomState && alive
		&& (((stateNo = 40 || stateNo = 42) && time >= 2)
			|| (stateNo != 40 && stateNo != 42)) {
	# The second half of that condition is the character's AI taking the jump
	# back off us: Honda cancels our State 40 into his own state 1200 on the very
	# next frame, and states 40 and 42 have ctrl = 0, so there is no ctrl to
	# remove that would stop him. Reaching here in any state other than the two
	# jump starts, inside the pin window, means exactly that - so launch anyway.
	map(_sfaz_pje_pin) := 0;
	# The pin is dropped unconditionally above, but the LAUNCH is skipped if the
	# state the AI took the jump back into is a hyper. +1 runs after the current
	# state, so that hyper's SuperPause has already spent the level by the time we
	# get here, and launching would bin it. Same reasoning as the jump-out guard
	# above and as the REACH_HYPER clause removed from data/ai_offense.zss.
	if stateNo != [3000, 4999] {
		if stateNo = 42 {
			playSnd{value: 40, 0; channel: 20}    # what state 42 plays on AnimTime = 0
		}
		velSet{x: map(_sfaz_pje_vx); y: const(velocity.jump.y)}
		changeState{value: 50; ctrl: 1}
	}
}


#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# AIR PUNISH - turn the evasion into an attack.
#
# Once the character is over the fireball and coming down on the person who threw
# it, it attacks instead of just landing: jump over the projectile and make them
# pay for throwing it. Only from AIR_PUNISH_AILEVEL up, and only during a jump
# this file asked for (map(_sfaz_pje_atk), set in the block above, which covers
# both the jumps it starts and the ones the character's own AI started on the
# same frame).
#
# Timed on the way DOWN (vel y > 0), which is where a jump-in connects: coming up,
# an air normal would whiff over their head and leave the character in recovery
# for the landing.
#
# The attack is picked from what the character actually has. 600/610/620 (air
# punches) and 630/640/650 (air kicks) are the roster's layout - all 46 have 600,
# 44 have all six; Juli and Juni only have 600, hence selfStateNoExist rather
# than a fixed number. The random ordering is only so the same character does not
# always answer with the same button.
#
#   AIR_PUNISH_AILEVEL   3   only above this AI level
#   AIR_PUNISH_RANGE    45   (240p) horizontal body distance to attack from
#   AIR_PUNISH_HEIGHT   30   (240p) minimum height, so it does not swing at the
#                            floor on the last frame before landing
#-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
if !isHelper && teamSide != 0 && aiLevel > 3 && gameTime < map(_sfaz_pje_atk)
		&& alive && !inCustomState && numEnemy != 0
		&& stateType = A && moveType = I && vel y > 0
		&& pos y <= const240p(-30)
		&& p2BodyDist x <= const240p(45) && p2BodyDist x >= const240p(-30) {

	let pjeAtk = 0;
	if random < 300 && selfStateNoExist(650) {
		let pjeAtk = 650;
	} else if random < 500 && selfStateNoExist(640) {
		let pjeAtk = 640;
	} else if random < 700 && selfStateNoExist(620) {
		let pjeAtk = 620;
	} else if selfStateNoExist(600) {
		let pjeAtk = 600;
	}

	if $pjeAtk != 0 {
		# Cleared BEFORE the ChangeState - a self ChangeState ends the state
		# block, so a line after it would never run.
		map(_sfaz_pje_atk) := 0;
		# Diagnostic latch, same idea as _sfaz_pje_fired: the gameTime of the
		# last air punish, so one of these can be told apart from the
		# character's own jump attack.
		map(_sfaz_pje_punished) := gameTime;
		changeState{value: $pjeAtk}
	}
}
